\SectionDivider{08}{Acquisition cost and incrementality}{Reconcile campaign credit with the no-campaign baseline, then match acquisition cost to the value relevant to the decision.}
\PageTitle{08 / CAC DEFINITIONS}{One acquisition ledger,\newline different decision quantities}{Observed cohort economics and the causal return to an acquisition intervention answer different questions.}
\Section{Observed blended CAC keeps the cohort accounts consistent}
\[
\text{Observed blended CAC}
=\frac{\text{recorded media + other acquisition spending}}{\text{all customers acquired in the matching cohort}}.
\]
This report's earlier cohort exhibits use this accounting quantity with the contribution of those same customers. The denominator includes buyers who might have joined without the tested campaign. It is not incrementality-based. No global holdout identifies incremental CAC for all 24 cohorts. Charging every buyer spend per incremental buyer would deduct more than the recorded spending.
\Section{Campaign credit is a starting point for reconciliation}
For each holdout test, a simple attribution-style benchmark credits every treated buyer to the tested campaign: $S_T/B_T$, where $S_T$ is treatment media spending and $B_T$ is treated buyers. This is a transparent treatment-arm benchmark, not a newly estimated last-click metric. It overstates the number of customers caused by the campaign when some would buy in its absence.
\Section{Incremental CAC removes the counterfactual baseline}
With $k=N_T/N_C$ to align assignment sizes,
\[
\Delta B=B_T-kB_C,\qquad \Delta S=S_T-kS_C,
\qquad \mathrm{iCAC}=\frac{\Delta S}{\Delta B}\quad(\Delta B>0).
\]
\SourceCite{larsen2019}{Larsen (2019)} motivates this distinction between attributed acquisitions, the background inflow and the return to additional spending. MBA 743 Topic 3, slides 6--10, develops the same cost-curve logic (\SourceCite{course2026}{Tian, 2026}).
\Boundary{Control estimates acquisitions without the tested campaign in its eligible population and window. That background may reflect prior brand exposure and other marketing; it is not a company-wide count of purely organic customers. Each test compares zero media with \$2.60 or \$3.60 per assigned prospect. Its average incremental CAC describes that dose, population and window; it establishes neither a marginal cost curve nor a channel ranking.}

\NewPage
\PageTitle{08 / CAC RECONCILIATION}{Holdouts remove background\newline acquisitions from campaign credit}{The existing synthetic experiments already randomize acquisition exposure. Their assignments, purchases and spending are preserved.}
\Section{From credited buyers to incremental buyers}
Each test assigns 250 prospects to campaign exposure and 250 to control. Search / save time is observed for May 1--June 1, 2025 (31 days); Partner / build skill for September 1--October 1 (30 days). End dates are exclusive. All assigned nonbuyers stay in the analysis.
\TableCaption{cac-buyers}{Control buyer counts estimate background acquisitions at the treatment-arm size. Both tests have $k=1$; the implementation also supports unequal arms.}
\VAR{cac_counts_table}
\TableCaption{cac-bridge}{The same media ledger with two acquisition denominators. Credited CAC is treatment media divided by all treated buyers; background share is estimated no-campaign buyers divided by treated buyers.}
\VAR{cac_costs_table}
\Evidence{Saved output: \Code{cac_reconciliation.csv}; source: \Code{experiment_summary.csv} and \Code{experiment_effects.csv}. Control media and other acquisition-cost entries for these tested campaigns are zero. The displayed estimates are media iCAC; a fully loaded decision must also include any additional acquisition costs. Currency rounds half up to cents.}
\Section{What changes in the reconciliation}
Search credits 73 buyers but induces an estimated 48; Partner credits 61 but induces 32. With zero control media spending, the correction multiplies credited CAC by $B_T/(B_T-B_C)$. If control spending is positive, both numerator and denominator must change; simply dividing attributed CAC by a lift fraction is insufficient.
\Boundary{The tested-program row sums these two separately standardized effects and their costs. It describes this particular two-test program across different populations and windows. It cannot calibrate all acquisition spending, establish a universal channel multiplier or replace whole-company blended CAC.}

\NewPage
\PageTitle{08 / EXPERIMENTAL UNCERTAINTY}{A precise cost ledger can\newline still produce an uncertain iCAC}{Buyer lift is estimated. Dividing by a small or uncertain lift can make acquisition cost highly uncertain.}
\TableCaption{cac-uncertainty}{Point estimates and approximate 95\% intervals. Buyer-lift intervals use independent randomized arms; iCAC intervals invert the positive lift interval with media spending fixed.}
\VAR{cac_intervals_table}
\Section{Keep the denominator's uncertainty visible}
For buyer probabilities $\hat p_T=B_T/N_T$ and $\hat p_C=B_C/N_C$,
\[
\operatorname{SE}(\Delta B)=N_T\sqrt{\frac{\hat p_T(1-\hat p_T)}{N_T}
+\frac{\hat p_C(1-\hat p_C)}{N_C}}.
\]
If the lift interval is $[L,U]$ with $L>0$, the corresponding iCAC interval is $[\Delta S/U,\Delta S/L]$. If the lift interval crosses zero, there is no finite upper iCAC bound over its positive region. A zero or negative point estimate of lift does not supply a meaningful positive acquisition-cost estimate.
\Evidence{These are unadjusted normal approximations, conditional on fixed media spending and independent assignment units. The program row sums separately standardized effects and, assuming independent tests, their variances. Shared audiences or overlapping interventions would require a joint design and a different covariance calculation.}
\Section{Incremental acquisitions do not guarantee a profitable campaign}
\VAR{cac_contribution_sentence} A bounded iCAC does not establish profit: Partner\textquotesingle s net-contribution interval includes zero. The following management exhibit retains both net-effect intervals and their 30/31-day horizons.
\Boundary{The control arms identify only the tested contrast. Estimating company-wide incremental CAC requires holdouts or coordinated experiments covering the relevant portfolio, with cross-channel overlap and substitution handled explicitly. Estimating a marginal cost curve additionally requires evidence across spending levels.}

\NewPage
\PageTitle{08 / CAC AND CUSTOMER VALUE}{Match acquisition cost\newline to the value being measured}{Use the same population, intervention, cost scope, time origin and horizon on both sides of the decision.}
\Section{For the acquired cohort: observed cost and cohort value}
The earlier exhibits combine average discounted contribution before CAC with observed blended CAC for the same acquired customers. Net cohort LTV subtracts that acquisition cost once. An LTV/CAC ratio uses value \emph{before} CAC in the numerator. Do not subtract iCAC from the lab's net LTV, or divide that net LTV by iCAC and interpret it as a causal acquisition return (\SourceCite{course2026}{Tian, 2026}, Topic 4, slides 3 and 31).
\Section{For an acquisition intervention: incremental value and cost}
Let $V_H$ be discounted contribution before acquisition cost for every assigned prospect over a prespecified horizon $H$ from assignment, including zeros. The campaign decision is
\[
\text{Incremental net value}_H
=N_T\bigl(\bar V_{T,H}-\bar V_{C,H}\bigr)-\Delta S_H.
\]
Cost must include all additional acquisition spending relevant to this contrast. Dividing incremental contribution and cost by the same positive $\Delta B$ gives a consistent per-incremental-acquisition comparison: $\Delta V_H-\Delta S_H=\Delta B(\Delta V_H/\Delta B-\mathrm{iCAC})$. Count buyers and value to the same $H$ under the same regime. However, that contribution ratio is the value of the intervention per additional acquisition; calling it the LTV of induced buyers requires assumptions, including monotone acquisition response and no change in spending or retention among people who would buy anyway.
\Section{What the current data can establish}
The existing experiments establish acquisition lift and short-run contribution effects. Controls are documented only through their prespecified 30/31-day windows. Later transactions do not establish continued control protection, so they do not identify a lifetime campaign effect. The cohort model forecasts twelve receipts from acquisition, a different clock from $H$ after assignment.
Multiplying conversion lift by average treated-buyer LTV would assume the unobserved induced buyers have that value and ignore possible effects on existing would-be buyers. The short-run effect is not a lower bound: pull-forward can let controls catch up, while retention can add value. A longer protected holdout could estimate discounted contribution for everyone assigned; extrapolation requires additional assumptions.
\Boundary{Use iCAC for the tested acquisition decision with matching incremental value. Keep observed blended CAC for the matching cohort accounts. The lab's cash-payback charts use \emph{undiscounted} contribution; MBA 743 Topic 4, slides 30 and 35, teaches discounted contribution payback. These are distinct conventions, and the existing cash figures retain their stated convention.}

\NewPage
\PageTitle{08 / CAMPAIGNS AND ACTION}{Campaign incrementality\newline and investment decisions}{Randomized assignment includes all eligible prospects, including those who never purchase. Each test keeps its own control and follow-up window.}
\TableCaption{experiment-arms}{Assigned prospects, buyers, recorded contribution and media spending by randomized arm.}
\VAR{experiment_table}
\ReportFigure{campaign-effects}{Randomized experiment. Incremental contribution after media per assigned prospect, with approximate 95\% intervals over each test's recorded follow-up window.}{experiment-effects.pdf}{1.6in}
\Evidence{Saved SQL outputs: \Code{experiment_summary.csv} and \Code{experiment_effects.csv}. Whiskers are approximate 95\% intervals from all-assigned contribution variances, including nonbuyer zeros. Fixed media costs shift the mean, not its sampling variance. These 30/31-day windows do not measure lifetime campaign return. Later transactions exist, but the exports do not document whether controls remained protected after the prespecified follow-up.}
\Section{The same economics apply to treatment and control}
For each arm, net revenue less recorded variable costs gives contribution. The difference in contribution per assigned eligible prospect is reduced by the treatment's additional media cost. Control purchases remain in the baseline rather than receiving campaign credit.
\[
\Delta\text{contribution after media per prospect}
=\frac{C_T-M_T}{N_T}-\frac{C_C-M_C}{N_C}.
\]
